Z||000000||BOILERMAKER||12-08-1999||10:57 AM||||I haven't bought the Haynes manual yet for the Fiero.  I've been using the service manual all this time and it has satisfied every question that I have, but now I am rebuilding the engine and I have more in-depth curiosities as to the gaskets and several other specific details.<P>Would the Haynes manual give me more information than the service manual gives???  How beneficial would the Haynes be if I already have the above material?  I believe I am going to buy one this afternoon, but wanted to know what to expect.  I didn't want to open it up this evening and find the same stuff that the service manual has.||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||12-08-1999||11:06 AM||||The Haynes manual doesn't have in depth detail.  Instead it shows you what steps are required to do stuff. I believe there are several chapter on engine rebuilding.  For both 4 and 6 cyl.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gokart||12-08-1999||09:08 PM||||Ya, it does. I have it. But if the directions are anything like the ones they gave me for replacing the oil pan gasket (ie:engine mount removal info left out) you might want to look elsewhere!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||12-09-1999||01:54 AM||||Both the Haynes and the Chiltons are geared more toward the shadetree mechanic, lots of how-to but they never go too in-depth.  I have both...||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||12-10-1999||12:48 AM||||I know the factory manual is expensive at $90. When things go well, it's a waste of money. But when things go bad, they are a god-send. ||||reg||1
